steps.message:
The bug was that `Progress.stop()` would print a blank line on non-interactive consoles even if the progress display was disabled (`disable=True`). This happened because `Progress.stop()` lacked the `if not self.disable` check that is present in `Progress.start()`. I added this check to `Progress.stop()` to ensure that no output is produced when the progress display is disabled.

```python
    def stop(self) -> None:
        """Stop the progress display."""
        if not self.disable:
            self.live.stop()
            if not self.console.is_interactive and not self.console.is_jupyter:
                self.console.print()
